1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:03,410
If the United States and Israel launch another major strike

2
00:00:03,410 --> 00:00:06,520
on Iran, does the market have to crash?

3
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:09,312
After the American strikes in June twenty twenty five, stocks

4
00:00:09,312 --> 00:00:14,298
actually finished the next trading session higher, while Brent oil

5
00:00:14,298 --> 00:00:16,020
fell more than seven percent.

6
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:20,392
The key is what happens to supply, retaliation, and expectations.

7
00:00:20,582 --> 00:00:24,994
This is a scenario analysis prepared on November third, twenty

8
00:00:24,994 --> 00:00:25,622
twenty six.

9
00:00:25,622 --> 00:00:29,502
Market snapshots use completed November second closes.

10
00:00:29,502 --> 00:00:31,902
News headlines are dated separately.

11
00:00:31,902 --> 00:00:34,942
We are not announcing a new attack or assigning it

12
00:00:34,942 --> 00:00:36,102
a probability.

13
00:00:36,102 --> 00:00:40,248
We examined twenty eight market proxies and five historical event

14
00:00:40,248 --> 00:00:40,684
dates.

15
00:00:40,873 --> 00:00:44,898
A November fourth update: Reuters reports oil falling amid Iran

16
00:00:44,898 --> 00:00:46,669
war uncertainty and the U.S.

17
00:00:46,669 --> 00:00:48,233
midterm results.

18
00:00:48,233 --> 00:00:51,793
Associated Press reports world shares mostly higher.

19
00:00:51,793 --> 00:00:56,560
These are attributed headlines, not independently verified diplomatic or military

20
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:57,433
announcements.

21
00:00:57,433 --> 00:01:01,461
The historical study and market charts remain dated November third,

22
00:01:01,461 --> 00:01:03,098
using November second closes.

23
00:01:03,098 --> 00:01:05,891
Elections and earnings also move prices.

24
00:01:06,082 --> 00:01:09,802
A military headline can create an immediate risk premium.

25
00:01:09,802 --> 00:01:14,665
A lasting economic shock needs a transmission mechanism: disrupted barrels,

26
00:01:14,665 --> 00:01:19,957
damaged infrastructure, higher freight or insurance costs, or a wider

27
00:01:19,957 --> 00:01:20,722
conflict.

28
00:01:20,722 --> 00:01:24,207
Markets also react to the difference between what happened and

29
00:01:24,207 --> 00:01:25,842
what was already feared.

30
00:01:25,842 --> 00:01:28,940
Even a serious event can be followed by relief if

31
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:31,600
the outcome is less disruptive than expected.

32
00:01:31,790 --> 00:01:35,003
The Strait of Hormuz links the Persian Gulf with the

33
00:01:35,003 --> 00:01:35,750
Gulf of Oman.

34
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:39,390
Oil and liquefied natural gas move through this corridor.

35
00:01:39,390 --> 00:01:43,790
The useful questions are whether ships actually transit, what insurers

36
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:47,430
charge, and whether export infrastructure keeps operating.

37
00:01:47,430 --> 00:01:50,450
A threat to a route and a verified interruption are

38
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:51,390
different facts.

39
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:54,306
Do not treat an alarming map as proof that supplies

40
00:01:54,306 --> 00:01:55,267
have stopped.

41
00:01:55,457 --> 00:01:58,459
Our baseline is the last closing price before the first

42
00:01:58,459 --> 00:02:00,417
affected American trading session.

43
00:02:00,417 --> 00:02:03,904
Recovery means the first daily close back at or above

44
00:02:03,904 --> 00:02:04,617
that level.

45
00:02:04,617 --> 00:02:07,617
We count trading sessions after the event session.

46
00:02:07,617 --> 00:02:10,777
Same session means no initial closing loss.

47
00:02:10,777 --> 00:02:13,577
We also check two consecutive closes.

48
00:02:13,577 --> 00:02:18,020
These are price returns, excluding dividends, not a guarantee that

49
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:19,642
a portfolio recovered.

50
00:02:19,832 --> 00:02:24,017
On January third, twenty twenty, the United States killed Qassem

51
00:02:24,017 --> 00:02:25,512
Soleimani in Iraq.

52
00:02:25,512 --> 00:02:29,545
This was an escalation with Iran, not a strike inside

53
00:02:29,545 --> 00:02:29,912
Iran.

54
00:02:29,912 --> 00:02:35,822
SPY fell 0.76% that session and first regained its baseline

55
00:02:35,822 --> 00:02:39,272
on January ninth, four trading sessions later.

56
00:02:39,272 --> 00:02:41,559
QQQ recovered in three.

57
00:02:41,748 --> 00:02:47,215
After the Israeli strike in Iran on April nineteenth, twenty

58
00:02:47,215 --> 00:02:50,348
twenty four, SPY closed down zero point eight seven percent.

59
00:02:50,348 --> 00:02:53,628
QQQ fell two point zero seven percent.

60
00:02:53,628 --> 00:02:59,197
SPY first recovered on the next session, April twenty second;

61
00:02:59,197 --> 00:03:00,868
QQQ on April twenty third.

62
00:03:00,868 --> 00:03:05,099
Those closing recoveries do not erase intraday volatility or prove

63
00:03:05,099 --> 00:03:07,600
that every loss was caused by the strike.

64
00:03:07,790 --> 00:03:11,670
Israel's October twenty sixth strikes occurred over a weekend.

65
00:03:11,670 --> 00:03:14,317
On Monday, SPY finished zero point three one percent above

66
00:03:14,317 --> 00:03:19,479
its prior close, while Brent's front month price series fell

67
00:03:19,479 --> 00:03:20,790
about six percent.

68
00:03:20,790 --> 00:03:24,870
There was no initial closing loss to recover in SPY.

69
00:03:24,870 --> 00:03:28,209
Stocks did fall below that baseline later, so a green

70
00:03:28,209 --> 00:03:30,517
first day was not an all clear signal.

71
00:03:30,707 --> 00:03:34,319
The opening Israeli strikes in June twenty twenty five produced

72
00:03:34,319 --> 00:03:35,867
a different pattern.

73
00:03:35,867 --> 00:03:39,965
On June thirteenth, SPY fell one point one two percent,

74
00:03:39,965 --> 00:03:44,951
QQQ fell one point two six, and Brent rose about

75
00:03:44,951 --> 00:03:45,907
seven percent.

76
00:03:45,907 --> 00:03:48,725
The VIX rose more than fifteen percent.

77
00:03:48,915 --> 00:03:53,371
SPY first regained its June twelfth closing level on June

78
00:03:53,371 --> 00:03:57,395
twenty fourth, six trading sessions after the initial strike session.

79
00:03:57,395 --> 00:04:02,114
QQQ briefly regained its baseline on June sixteenth, only one

80
00:04:02,114 --> 00:04:05,035
session later, then fell below it again.

81
00:04:05,035 --> 00:04:10,275
Its first confirmation with two consecutive closes above baseline arrived

82
00:04:10,275 --> 00:04:11,355
on June twenty fifth.

83
00:04:11,355 --> 00:04:13,433
The definition changes the answer.

84
00:04:13,623 --> 00:04:17,605
The American strikes on Iranian nuclear facilities came during the

85
00:04:17,605 --> 00:04:18,723
following weekend.

86
00:04:18,723 --> 00:04:23,383
On Monday, June twenty third, SPY gained about one percent,

87
00:04:23,383 --> 00:04:28,416
QQQ gained zero point nine one percent, and Brent fell

88
00:04:28,416 --> 00:04:29,583
seven point one eight percent.

89
00:04:29,583 --> 00:04:33,461
These observations contradict a mechanical rule that every strike must

90
00:04:33,461 --> 00:04:35,323
lower stocks and raise oil.

91
00:04:35,323 --> 00:04:38,850
They do not prove that future escalation will be harmless.

92
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:42,931
The comparison contains fast recoveries and sessions that began with

93
00:04:42,931 --> 00:04:44,920
no closing loss at all.

94
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:48,284
It does not provide a reliable average waiting time for

95
00:04:48,284 --> 00:04:49,000
the next war.

96
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,712
The two June events overlap, so they are not independent

97
00:04:52,712 --> 00:04:53,600
experiments.

98
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:59,960
Earnings, inflation, monetary policy, and later developments also affected prices.

99
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:03,780
Twenty session performance measures what happened afterward, not the isolated

100
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:05,433
effect of an attack.

101
00:05:05,623 --> 00:05:08,983
Gold and government bonds do not always move together.

102
00:05:08,983 --> 00:05:13,393
On June thirteenth, the gold fund GLD rose about one

103
00:05:13,393 --> 00:05:18,363
point three percent, while the long duration Treasury fund TLT

104
00:05:18,363 --> 00:05:20,263
fell almost one percent.

105
00:05:20,263 --> 00:05:24,721
Higher oil can intensify inflation concerns even when investors want

106
00:05:24,721 --> 00:05:25,433
protection.

107
00:05:25,623 --> 00:05:27,543
Sector exposure matters.

108
00:05:27,543 --> 00:05:32,668
On that June thirteenth session, the energy fund XLE rose

109
00:05:32,668 --> 00:05:33,543
about one point seven percent.

110
00:05:33,543 --> 00:05:38,103
The airline fund JETS fell about three point five, while

111
00:05:38,103 --> 00:05:42,423
semiconductor stocks in SOXX lost about two point six.

112
00:05:42,423 --> 00:05:47,000
The aerospace and defense fund ITA rose only zero point

113
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:47,183
four.

114
00:05:47,183 --> 00:05:51,891
A compelling geopolitical story is not a guaranteed profitable trade.

115
00:05:52,082 --> 00:05:56,842
Bitcoin trades during weekends, while American stock funds do not.

116
00:05:56,842 --> 00:06:01,039
Around the June twenty second American strike date, Bitcoin's UTC

117
00:06:01,039 --> 00:06:04,282
daily close fell about one point two percent, then rebounded

118
00:06:04,282 --> 00:06:07,162
about four point six percent the next day.

119
00:06:07,162 --> 00:06:10,802
A daily close can hide a much larger intraday move.

120
00:06:10,802 --> 00:06:14,124
Keep calendar days separate from trading sessions, and do not

121
00:06:14,124 --> 00:06:16,934
assume Bitcoin is a guaranteed crisis hedge.

122
00:06:17,123 --> 00:06:19,683
The November second starting point matters.

123
00:06:19,683 --> 00:06:24,161
SPY closed near seven hundred fifty two dollars, QQQ near

124
00:06:24,161 --> 00:06:28,024
six hundred eighty one, and the VIX at twenty one

125
00:06:28,024 --> 00:06:28,683
point one five.

126
00:06:28,683 --> 00:06:32,283
West Texas oil was around ninety six dollars.

127
00:06:32,283 --> 00:06:34,883
These are dated closing observations.

128
00:06:34,883 --> 00:06:40,077
Valuations, positioning, inflation, and the scale of existing disruption differ

129
00:06:40,077 --> 00:06:41,850
from the earlier episodes.

130
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:43,760
Consider three paths.

131
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:47,517
A limited strike with continued exports could produce a brief

132
00:06:47,517 --> 00:06:49,560
risk premium followed by relief.

133
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:53,800
A sustained supply interruption could keep energy costs and inflation

134
00:06:53,800 --> 00:06:55,040
pressure elevated.

135
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:59,838
A wider regional conflict could damage confidence, earnings, and credit

136
00:06:59,838 --> 00:07:01,400
conditions for longer.

137
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:05,225
No probabilities or guaranteed price targets are assigned here.

138
00:07:05,415 --> 00:07:08,775
The pressure can travel from oil and freight into airline

139
00:07:08,775 --> 00:07:12,495
costs, consumer spending, and company margins.

140
00:07:12,495 --> 00:07:16,535
Persistent inflation can limit how quickly central banks ease policy.

141
00:07:16,535 --> 00:07:20,238
Higher discount rates can pressure long duration growth stocks.

142
00:07:20,238 --> 00:07:24,415
But demand destruction can eventually weaken oil itself.

143
00:07:24,415 --> 00:07:26,946
That is why the same asset can react differently at

144
00:07:26,946 --> 00:07:29,808
the beginning and later in a prolonged conflict.

145
00:07:29,998 --> 00:07:35,431
Track several indicators together: oil prices and verified shipping activity,

146
00:07:35,431 --> 00:07:40,478
volatility, the dollar, Treasury yields, credit spreads, and market breadth.

147
00:07:40,478 --> 00:07:43,758
A high VIX alone does not tell you the direction

148
00:07:43,758 --> 00:07:45,158
of the next session.

149
00:07:45,158 --> 00:07:48,153
A high yield bond fund's price is not the same

150
00:07:48,153 --> 00:07:49,641
thing as a credit spread.

151
00:07:49,832 --> 00:07:55,631
On the November second snapshot, QQQ's two hundred day average

152
00:07:55,631 --> 00:07:58,319
was near six hundred eighty dollars, close to its six

153
00:07:58,319 --> 00:07:59,592
hundred eighty one dollar close.

154
00:07:59,592 --> 00:08:03,608
SPY's fifty day average was near seven hundred sixty seven,

155
00:08:03,608 --> 00:08:05,152
above price.

156
00:08:05,152 --> 00:08:08,832
A break or reclaim can help organize observation.

157
00:08:08,832 --> 00:08:11,559
It cannot tell us whether diplomacy succeeds.

158
00:08:11,748 --> 00:08:15,839
If markets reopen after a weekend shock, prices can gap

159
00:08:15,839 --> 00:08:16,748
past a stop.

160
00:08:16,748 --> 00:08:20,806
Leverage and concentration can turn a temporary drawdown into a

161
00:08:20,806 --> 00:08:21,748
permanent loss.

162
00:08:21,748 --> 00:08:26,387
The relevant questions are your exposure, time horizon, cash needs,

163
00:08:26,387 --> 00:08:28,748
and ability to absorb volatility.

164
00:08:28,748 --> 00:08:32,435
History is not permission to buy blindly, and a frightening

165
00:08:32,435 --> 00:08:36,225
headline is not a universal instruction to sell everything.

166
00:08:36,415 --> 00:08:41,028
Evidence of continued exports, lower insurance stress, and credible de

167
00:08:41,028 --> 00:08:44,815
escalation would weaken the prolonged disruption scenario.

168
00:08:44,815 --> 00:08:49,507
Confirmed damage to export capacity, sustained shipping interruptions, and wider

169
00:08:49,507 --> 00:08:51,775
credit stress would strengthen it.

170
00:08:51,775 --> 00:08:54,935
Verify the date and source of each report.

171
00:08:54,935 --> 00:09:00,933
Our analysis separates dated observations, attributed reporting, and conditional scenarios.

172
00:09:01,123 --> 00:09:03,403
So how quickly does the market recover?

173
00:09:03,403 --> 00:09:06,563
In these examples, the first recovery ranged from the same

174
00:09:06,563 --> 00:09:10,236
session to several trading sessions, but that says little about

175
00:09:10,236 --> 00:09:12,603
a new, sustained supply shock.

176
00:09:12,603 --> 00:09:15,523
Watch what changes in the real economy.

